    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Deformation bands (DBs) are thin, tabular, bounded features of localized granular shear and/or compaction that commonly occur as systematic and pervasive arrays in porous sandstone. Their gross morphology strongly resembles that of joints and faults, suggesting that many key aspects of DB behavior—propagation, interaction and pattern development—might also be understood within the unifying context of linear elastic fracture mechanics. In this paper, I present a conceptual and quantitative framework for pursuing this approach, and propose an integrated program of field mapping, thin-section analysis and boundary element numerical modeling to test, develop and refine it.\r\n\r\nA simple 2-D analysis of isolated end-member DB types suggests that the general tendency to propagate in-plane is compatible with their perturbations of the near-tip stress field. In particular, pronounced fluctuations in stress state experienced as a DB tip passes could create a “cloud” of disaggregated sand grains ripe for reorganization via shear and compaction. It also seems clear that DBs with net shear might be expected to exhibit signs of an asymmetric stress overprinting along their outer flanks—tensional on one side and compressional on the other.\r\n\r\nThese preliminary theoretical observations will guide the initial stages of my field and lab efforts to provide realistic physical parameters and constraints for the modeling effort, which should yield valuable insights into the relative influences of material property, geometric and remote loading factors in DB mechanics. Ultimately, the results of this effort should prove useful in assessing the presence, distribution and geometry of low-permeability DB arrays in active subsurface sandstone reservoirs and aquifers, where they have the potential to pose substantial impediments to fluid flow.",
